`,s.jsx(r,{items:[{q:"Jacobian or Hessian — which for a layer mapping R^512 to R^256, and which for the scalar loss?",a:"The layer is vector-to-vector, so its matrix of first partials is a Jacobian (256 x 512). The loss is vector-to-scalar, so its matrix of second partials is a Hessian (over the parameters). Backprop chains Jacobians; curvature analysis uses the Hessian."},{q:"Why does the saddle problem get worse as dimensions increase?",a:"A critical point is a minimum only if EVERY Hessian eigenvalue is positive. Treating the signs as roughly independent, the chance of all n being positive falls off exponentially in n. With millions of parameters, virtually every flat point has at least one downhill direction — it is a saddle."},{q:"Newton's method converges in fewer steps. Why does nobody use it to train a large network?",a:"It needs the Hessian and its inverse. For n parameters that is n^2 entries and roughly n^3 work to invert — with n in the millions, forming the matrix alone is impossible in memory, never mind inverting it each step."},{q:"Write the second-order Taylor expansion of a loss and say what each term does.",a:"f(theta + d) ≈ f(theta) + grad^T d + (1/2) d^T H d. The first term is the current loss, the second is the linear slope that plain gradient descent uses, and the third is the curvature correction — it is what tells you whether a step of a given size will overshoot."}]}),`
